=== HINT 1: fork ===
pid_t child = fork();
// child == 0 in child process, >0 (child PID) in parent, -1 on error

=== HINT 2: execvp in child ===
char *args[] = { "ls", "-la", "/tmp", NULL };
execvp(args[0], args);
perror("execvp");  // only reached if exec fails
_exit(127);

=== HINT 3: waitpid in parent ===
int status;
waitpid(child, &status, 0);

=== HINT 4: Check exit status ===
if (WIFEXITED(status))
    printf("exited: %d\n", WEXITSTATUS(status));
else if (WIFSIGNALED(status))
    printf("signal: %d\n", WTERMSIG(status));

=== KEY INSIGHT ===
fork() is copy-on-write - the child shares all pages until one side writes.
execve() replaces the process image: code, data, stack, heap all replaced.
Only file descriptors (unless O_CLOEXEC), signal handlers, and credentials survive exec.
posix_spawn() is a combined fork+exec for performance (avoids forking large processes).
vfork() is an older optimization: child borrows parent's stack, must exec or _exit immediately.
